userStories.description:
**Goal** — Repaint the full alternate-screen chat frame once after a burst of terminal resizes, closing #2973.

**Motivation** — After the window was resized while a Bash tool ran, the screen showed `Running Bash… (1255s …)` above `Running Bash… (1263s …)`, with an extra set of divider rules and an extra prompt row.

**Approach** —
*Ink patch.* `patches/ink@6.7.0.patch` (root `patchedDependencies`) keeps its existing `log-update.js` `cursorUp` hunk and gains these additions to ink 6.7.0's `build/` files:

- `log-update.js`: both `createStandard` and `createIncremental` get a `render.reset` that forgets the previous frame without writing anything and without touching the cursor's visibility:
  ```js
  // createStandard
  render.reset = () => {
      previousOutput = '';
      previousLineCount = 0;
      previousCursorPosition = undefined;
      cursorWasShown = false;
  };
  // createIncremental
  render.reset = () => {
      previousOutput = '';
      previousLines = [];
      previousCursorPosition = undefined;
      cursorWasShown = false;
  };
  ```
- `ink.js`: a `repaint` method on `Ink`:
  ```js
  repaint() {
      if (this.isUnmounted) {
          return;
      }
      this.log.reset();
      this.lastOutput = '';
      this.lastOutputToRender = '';
      this.onRender();
  }
  ```
- `render.js`: the returned instance gains `repaint: () => { instance.repaint(); }`.
- `render.d.ts`: `Instance` gains `repaint: () => void;` with the doc comment `Forget the drawn frame and write the whole current frame again.`

The patch is regenerated with `bun patch ink@6.7.0` (it prints the directory to edit) and `bun patch --commit <that directory>`, then re-applied with `bun install`. Ink stays pinned at 6.7.0 (`check-bundle-externals` rule 5).

*renderChat.* `tui/chat/alt-screen.ts` adds:

```ts
/** Erase the whole alternate screen and home the cursor, so a full frame redraws from 1:1. */
export const CLEAR_ALT_SCREEN = "\u001b[2J\u001b[H";
```

It is re-exported from the `tui/chat/index.ts` barrel beside `ENTER_ALT_SCREEN`. `tui/chat/index.ts` exports `RESIZE_REPAINT_MS = 50` and adds one dependency to `_renderChatDeps`:

```ts
/** Subscribe to terminal resizes; the returned remover unsubscribes. */
onResize: (handler: () => void): (() => void) => {
  process.stdout.on("resize", handler);
  return () => {
    process.stdout.off("resize", handler);
  };
},
```

After `_renderChatDeps.render` returns, `renderChat` subscribes once with `_renderChatDeps.onResize`. Each resize event, while the screen is not restored, clears a pending repaint handle with `_renderChatDeps.clearTimeout` and arms a new one with `_renderChatDeps.setTimeout(repaint, RESIZE_REPAINT_MS)`, so a burst of resize events repaints once, after the last one; by then `ChatApp`'s own resize listener has committed the frame at the new size. `repaint` does nothing once restored; otherwise it calls `_renderChatDeps.write(CLEAR_ALT_SCREEN)` and then `instance.repaint()`, in that order. `instance.clear()` and `instance.rerender()` are not used for this.

`restore()` calls the resize remover once and, only when a repaint handle is armed, clears it with `_renderChatDeps.clearTimeout`; with none armed it makes no `clearTimeout` call, so the existing exact-order assertions in `index.test.tsx` keep holding. The repaint handle is a separate variable from the title-animation `timer`; neither path clears the other's handle. The repaint uses `setTimeout` rather than `Bun.sleep` because the handle must be cancellable (the `forbidden-patterns-source` exception); the call site says so in a comment, as `armFrame` does.

*Real-terminal check.* `test/integration/tui/chat/resize.test.ts` drives the real `renderChat` in tmux against an unresized reference pane. Its fixture (`test/fixtures/chat-pty/host.ts`) captures only bytes Ink writes through its stream, so `CLEAR_ALT_SCREEN`, written by `_renderChatDeps.write`, never reaches its `/bytes` check for `\x1b[2J`; its existing cases keep their assertions. US-001 adds one width case to it. tmux may not re-wrap the alternate screen when it narrows, so that case may pass before the fix as well: it is a regression guard, and the real-Ink fixture is the proof that `repaint` writes every row.

**Test placement and seams** — US-001 adds its `renderChat` tests to `test/ui/chat/index.test.tsx`, its real-Ink repaint test to `test/ui/chat/ink-incremental-render.test.tsx` with a new fixture `test/fixtures/ink-incremental/repaint.mjs` (modelled on `two-frames.mjs`), and its PTY width case to `test/integration/tui/chat/resize.test.ts`. A file that would pass 650 lines is split by describe block into `<module>-<concern>.test.ts(x)`. The `index.test.tsx` mount helper stubs `_renderChatDeps.onResize` with one that records the handler and returns a recording remover, and its fake instance gains a recording `repaint`; its existing `afterEach` restore covers the stub, and every existing expectation is unchanged. The default onResize is tested separately without leaking a listener. The unit tests mount through renderChat and fire the recorded handler, the real-Ink fixture proves a full redraw of unchanged rows, and tmux exercises the default subscription. Show the unit/real-Ink regression failing against old code; tmux alone is not that proof.

**Failure handling** — A repaint timer fires after `restore()`: it calls neither `_renderChatDeps.write` nor `instance.repaint()`. `Ink.repaint()` after unmount: returns without writing.

**Scope** — In: the existing root Ink patch and necessary generated lockfile update, alternate-screen clear constant, debounced renderChat repaint and cleanup, and the specified tests/fixture. Out: US-002 duration formatting and US-003 task-notice steering. Editing `tui/chat/ChatApp.tsx`: it is over the 600-line source cap and may not grow; US-001 works in `renderChat` and US-003 in `useJobs`. Changing Ink's own `resized` handler, upgrading Ink, or widening its pin: the patch only adds `reset` and `repaint`. Repainting on anything other than a resize: status and stream ticks keep incremental rendering.

**Interface** —
```ts
// Ink Instance addition, through build/render.d.ts in the patch:
/** Forget the drawn frame and write the whole current frame again. */
repaint: () => void;
```
The CLEAR_ALT_SCREEN and onResize declarations and RESIZE_REPAINT_MS value above are the target additions; renderChat continues returning Ink's Instance.

**Goal** — Send a completed background-task notice into the running native turn and acknowledge it only when that turn takes the steer, avoiding a duplicate follow-up turn and closing #2975.

**Motivation** — When the turn ended, `useJobs` delivered `[nax-tasks] background command <id> (...) ended: ...` as a new turn, and the agent re-reported a result it had already handled.

**Approach** — `useJobs` (`tui/chat/use-jobs.ts`) gains a steer path for background task notices only; job notices and run-ask notices keep today's delivery. It keeps two refs: a pending flag, and the refused notice text.

On each render where `idle` is true, the refused text is cleared first. On each render where `idle` is false:

1. If a steer is pending, or a delivery from the idle path is in flight (`inFlight.current`, which stays set until the notice turn it started has settled and `takeNotice` has run), or `host.steer` is undefined, or `tasks?.peekNotice()` returns `undefined`, do nothing. A notice turn therefore never steers its own notice into itself.
2. If the head notice text equals the refused text, do nothing.
3. Otherwise call `host.steer(notice)` once:
   - `accepted`: set the pending flag. When `applied` resolves `true`, call `tasks.takeNotice()` and `note(steeredTaskNoticeLine(summary))`, where `summary` is `summarizeBackgroundNotice(notice)` from `@/prompts`, or `background task notice` when it returns `undefined`. When `applied` resolves `false` or rejects, store the notice text as refused and leave the notice queued, so the same notice is not steered again before the chat is idle. Either way clear the pending flag and, while mounted, refresh, so the next head is considered.
   - `unsupported` or `no_turn`: store the notice text as refused; leave the notice queued.

The idle path (today's delivery as a new turn) keeps its order: run asks, then job notices, then task notices. While the pending flag is set it skips only the task source; a run ask or a job notice is still delivered. A task notice skipped because a steer is pending is not the guard's hold: it does not add the `taskNoticeHeldLine` row and does not set `heldAnnounced`. A steered notice does not count toward the consecutive task-turn guard (`taskTurns`) and does not reset it; it is not a turn. Steering also happens when `taskNoticeTurns` is `0`: that setting stops task notices from starting turns, and a steer starts none. A native turn closes its steer channel when it settles (`steer-channel.ts`: pending steers resolve `false`), so a pending flag clears when the turn it was steered into ends.

```ts
/** The transcript row for a task notice the running turn took as a steer. */
export function steeredTaskNoticeLine(summary: string): string {
  return `${summary} · sent into the running turn`;
}
```

`ChatApp.tsx` is not edited: `useJobs` already receives `host`, and `note` already appends a notice row.

**Existing integration contracts (read-only)** —
- `ChatHost.steer?(text: string): SteerResult` (`packages/nax/src/chat/chat-host-types.ts:98`).
- `SteerResult` = `{ status: "accepted"; steerId: string; applied: Promise<boolean> } | { status: "unsupported" } | { status: "no_turn" }` (`packages/nax-agent/src/session/steer-channel.ts:24`). `applied` resolves `true` when the loop took the text and `false` when the turn ended with it pending.
- `ChatBackgroundTasks.peekNotice()` / `takeNotice()` (`chat/background/task-types.ts:61`): `peekNotice` returns a stable snapshot until `takeNotice` consumes exactly those tasks.
- `summarizeBackgroundNotice(text): string | undefined` (`prompts/builders/notice-summary.ts:63`, exported from `@/prompts`).

**Failure handling** — `host.steer` throws: `useJobs` logs a warning (`getLogger()?.warn("chat", "Background notice steer failed; notice retained", { error })`), stores the notice text as refused, and leaves the notice queued; no steer is pending. `applied` rejects: treated as `false`: the notice text is stored as refused, the notice stays queued, the pending flag clears, and a warning is logged with the same message. `applied` never settles: only task notices wait; run asks and job notices are still delivered when idle.

**Test placement and seams** — US-003 adds its tests to `test/ui/chat/task-notices.test.tsx`; its local `mount()` gains a `steer` parameter passed through to `mountChatPicker`. A file that would pass 650 lines is split by describe block into `<module>-<concern>.test.ts(x)`. `test/helpers/chat-picker.tsx` — `MountOptions` has no `steer`, so a test cannot give the fake host a steerable turn; `MountOptions` gains `steer?: ChatHost["steer"]`, the fake host spreads it in only when given, and a mount without it builds the same host as before. US-003's ACs mount the chat screen with a fake host whose `steer` is a recording stub, start a user turn, end a background task, and assert the call, including no repeat call on later renders. Use deferred applied/send promises and real background registries through the existing helper, preserving all current no-steer, notice order and guard assertions. Show the regression failing against old useJobs before implementation.

**Scope** — In: useJobs steering/refusal/acknowledgement lifecycle and task-only pending wait, steeredTaskNoticeLine, optional steer wiring in the existing test helper, and UI integration regressions. Out: US-001 resize and Ink changes; US-002 duration formatting. Steering for ACP sessions: they have no mid-turn input (`steer` answers `unsupported`), so a task that ends during an ACP turn still starts a new turn after it, as today. Steering job notices and run-ask notices: only background task notices take the steer path. Marking a task seen when the agent reads its log or output files with Bash or another tool instead of `nax-tasks__output`. Editing `tui/chat/ChatApp.tsx`: it is over the 600-line source cap and may not grow; US-001 works in `renderChat` and US-003 in `useJobs`. The nax-agent steer channel, task registry and notice-summary builder remain read-only contracts.

**Interface** —
```ts
// Existing host capability, consumed unchanged:
steer?(text: string): SteerResult;
// Existing result contract:
// { status: "accepted"; steerId: string; applied: Promise<boolean> }
// | { status: "unsupported" } | { status: "no_turn" }
// New transcript helper:
export function steeredTaskNoticeLine(summary: string): string {
  return `${summary} · sent into the running turn`;
}
// Existing test helper's MountOptions addition:
steer?: ChatHost["steer"];
```
